desktop-shell: snap windows to the top of a screen; fixes for several screens

Window snapping, like Windows:
- Dragging a window to the top edge of a screen shows a translucent
  preview of that screen's work area; dropping it there maximizes the
  window on that screen. Restoring it later goes back to where the drag
  started (when that was on the same screen).
- Dragging a maximized window (its title bar, or Super + drag) restores
  it once the pointer has moved a few pixels, and the restored window
  stays under the pointer at the same place of its title bar, so the
  drag goes on. A click or double click does not restore it.

Several screens:
- A new window bigger than the work area of its screen (e.g. it
  remembered its size from a bigger or less scaled screen) is asked to
  fit the screen and is centered once its new size arrives. New windows
  are also placed by their visible size (without client-side shadows),
  inside the work area.
- desktop-shell client: the taskbar and wallpaper of a screen could stay
  at scale 1 on a scaled screen: the screen's scale event may come
  before they are created, and was then never applied. A scale change
  now also redraws them, instead of leaving the old buffer with the new
  scale.

